      "parent_index": null,
      "last_updated": "2026-05-12T00:00:00.000000",
      "title_align": "left",
      "section": "Steps",
      "order": 6,
      "alignment": "left",
      "vertical_alignment": "top",
      "body": "The history of an image registration, read-only: what was done to each sample, one step at a time, as the processing tool leaves it (the uncorrected image, then a rigid, an affine and a B-spline transform). Pick a step in the history beside the image, or play it: the tile shows that step's output next to the step before (or the fixed image), at the same zoom, with the parameters it ran with and whether each metric got better or worse. Nothing here reruns or tunes a step; that is elastix's or ANTs' job. Two synthetic samples, so the true transform is known: embryo_a improves at every step; embryo_b gets worse at the affine step (the shear is fitted the wrong way round), and its B-spline step cannot fully recover and folds the image in one place. The parameters are those an elastix run of each step would use; the outputs are simulated (dev/bioimage/make_registration_example.py).\n",
      "surface": "none",
      "accent": null,
      "logo": null,
      "logo_dark": null,
      "wf_tag": "python/bioimage_demo"
    },
